<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <groupId>com.example</groupId>
  <artifactId>myapp</artifactId>
  <version>0.1.0</version>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>myapp</name>

  <properties>
    <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
    <maven.compiler.release>25</maven.compiler.release>
    <main.class>com.example.Main</main.class>
    <panamaview.version>1.0.0</panamaview.version>
  </properties>

  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.github.lihuu</groupId>
      <artifactId>panamaview</artifactId>
      <version>${panamaview.version}</version>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>3.13.0</version>
      </plugin>

      <!--
        Two JVM flags, both compulsory, neither optional.

        The second grants native access, since the calls into Cocoa and WebKit are native.
        Without it the JVM warns on every one of them.

        The first is macOS's alone and comes from ${first.thread.flag} below. It keeps AppKit
        happy: it requires the event loop to own the process' first thread, and the java
        launcher hands main() a freshly created one. Without it the window misbehaves in ways
        that never name the cause.

        On any other platform the same flag is not ignored — the JVM refuses to start at all
        ("Unrecognized option: -XstartOnFirstThread"). That is why it is a property and not a
        literal: there is no "harmless everywhere" form of it. A GraalVM native executable does
        not need it on any platform, because its main thread already is the first thread.

        exec:exec rather than exec:java: the app has to own the process' first thread, and
        that can only be arranged at JVM start-up.

        (No double dashes anywhere in this comment: XML forbids them, and Maven fails the
        whole build with a parse error rather than ignoring it.)
      -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
        <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>3.5.0</version>
        <configuration>
          <executable>${java.home}/bin/java</executable>
          <!-- What every platform but macOS runs. The macOS profile below replaces this list
               wholesale, because Maven merges plugin configuration by element and there is no way
               to say "this argument, only here". -->
          <arguments>
            <argument>--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED</argument>
            <!-- There was a ${panamaview.jvmArgs} argument here, to let
                 `mvn exec:exec -Dpanamaview.check=true` reach the JVM the way JAVA_OPTS does for
                 ./run.sh. It broke the plain command instead: an unset property does NOT disappear;
                 it is passed through as an *empty* argument, and java takes the empty string for the
                 main class. Measured: the argument list came out with an empty element between
                 the native-access flag and -cp, and the build died with "Could not find or load
                 main class", on macOS and Windows
                 alike. No verification script used this path, which is why nothing caught it.
                 ./run.sh is how a non-default JVM flag is passed; this plugin runs the default. -->
            <argument>-cp</argument>
            <classpath/>
            <argument>${main.class}</argument>
          </arguments>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>

  <!--
    -XstartOnFirstThread is macOS's alone, and it has to be added by a profile because there is no
    "harmless elsewhere" spelling of it. The JVM treats an unknown -X option as a fatal start-up
    error — measured on Windows 11 with JDK 25: "Unrecognized option: -XstartOnFirstThread",
    "Could not create the Java Virtual Machine" — so a pom carrying it on Windows or Linux does not
    warn, it does not run.

    Not a property either, which is what this replaced. A property set to empty reads as "no flag",
    but exec-maven-plugin passes an empty <argument/> through as an *empty argument*: the command
    line came out with a bare comma in it, and java then took the empty string for the main class —
    "Could not find or load main class". A subtler failure than the one being fixed, and it was
    measured rather than reasoned about.

    A profile is the only tool Maven has for adding one argument to a list under a condition, so
    that is what adds it. Everything else stays put.
  -->
  <profiles>
    <profile>
      <id>macos-first-thread</id>
      <activation>
        <os>
          <family>mac</family>
        </os>
      </activation>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
            <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            <configuration>
              <arguments>
                <argument>-XstartOnFirstThread</argument>
                <argument>--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED</argument>
                <argument>-cp</argument>
                <classpath/>
                <argument>${main.class}</argument>
              </arguments>
            </configuration>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>
  </profiles>

</project>
